% ============================================================================
% wave-types — each of several named waves and the WAVE FAMILY it belongs to,
% mechanical or electromagnetic (adj-facts-stdlib, PHYSICS).
% ============================================================================
% An elementary/middle-school physics fact on the way to waves and optics:
% every wave you meet is one of two great families. A MECHANICAL wave carries
% energy by shaking matter, so it needs a medium (water, air, a rope). An
% ELECTROMAGNETIC wave carries energy in oscillating electric and magnetic
% fields, so it needs no medium at all and can cross the vacuum of space. Recall
% is a binding query:
%
%     ? wave_family(sound, $F)   % mechanical
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `wave_family(wave, family)` carrying the table's citation, so the
% lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the family
% WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains on anything
% the source does not classify.
%
% The named waves, as a little truth table (wave → the family NASA places it in):
%
%     wave            family            why (which sentence names it)
%     -------------   ---------------   -----------------------------------------
%     water           mechanical        "…examples of mechanical waves."
%     sound           mechanical        "…examples of mechanical waves."
%     rope            mechanical        "…waves on a rope … mechanical waves."
%     gamma           electromagnetic   "…all forms of electromagnetic radiation:
%                                        gamma rays…"
%     xray            electromagnetic   "…X-rays…"
%     ultraviolet     electromagnetic   "…ultraviolet light…"
%     visible_light   electromagnetic   "…visible light…"
%     infrared        electromagnetic   "…infrared light…"
%     microwave       electromagnetic   "…microwaves…"
%     radio           electromagnetic   "…radio waves."
%
% This is the concrete rung a physics learner reasons UP from: before reasoning
% about wavelength, frequency, refraction, or the Doppler effect, a student first
% learns to SORT a wave into its family. The query also runs BACKWARD — bind the
% family and recall a wave that belongs to it:
%
%     ? wave_family($W, electromagnetic)   % radio (or another EM wave) — reverse
%
% Something the source never sorts into a family — a seismic wave, a gravitational
% wave — has no row, so a recall on it ABSTAINS rather than inventing a family.
% That honest-abstention property is what makes the table safe to reason from.
%
% Each `family` value is one of the two SINGLE lowercase atoms the source itself
% uses to name the two families — `mechanical` and `electromagnetic` — copied
% verbatim from the sentence that classifies each wave; never a family the source
% does not assign. Each `wave` key is a faithful short snake_case paraphrase of a
% wave the source explicitly names (gamma rays → gamma, X-rays → xray, ultraviolet
% light → ultraviolet, visible light → visible_light, infrared light → infrared,
% microwaves → microwave, radio waves → radio).
%
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Provenance (nothing here is asserted from memory — every wave→family pair was
% read out of a fetched NASA education page this session, and any wave that could
% not be grounded there would have been dropped). All rows come from the SAME
% primary U.S. government source, NASA Science's James Webb Space Telescope
% education asset "Electromagnetic Wave (Light Wave) vs. Mechanical Wave"
% (science.nasa.gov/asset/webb/electromagnetic-wave-light-wave-vs-mechanical-wave/),
% quoted here character-for-character so any reader can re-check it.
%
% The mechanical family (water, sound, rope) is fixed by ONE sentence —
%
%   "Water waves, sound waves, and waves on a rope are all examples of
%    mechanical waves."
%
% The electromagnetic family (gamma, xray, ultraviolet, visible_light, infrared,
% microwave, radio) is fixed by ONE sentence —
%
%   "Light waves include all forms of electromagnetic radiation: gamma rays,
%    X-rays, ultraviolet light, visible light, infrared light, microwaves, and
%    radio waves."
%
% (and the same page names light itself as electromagnetic: "Light waves, also
% called electromagnetic waves, involve oscillations of electric and magnetic
% fields rather than oscillations of matter.")
%
% EXTENDED this cycle from 7 to 10 rows: the SAME already-quoted electromagnetic
% sentence above always named SEVEN electromagnetic waves (gamma rays, X-rays,
% ultraviolet light, visible light, infrared light, microwaves, and radio
% waves), but only four had ever been turned into a row -- ultraviolet,
% infrared, and microwave were sitting in this table's own header the whole
% time. Zero new WebFetch needed to discover them, mirroring `synonyms.adj`'s
% and `opposites.adj`'s header-only extension pattern; this cycle it applies to
% adding new KEYS (more waves) rather than more values per key.
%
% EVERY ROW CARRIES ITS OWN SPAN, below, in the row's `{ ... }` block
% (ADJ-TABLES RS-5e). TWO sentences for ten rows, both on the one NASA page:
% the mechanical caption names water, sound and rope; the electromagnetic
% caption names all seven bands.
%
% WHAT #14986 CHANGED. The MECHANICAL membership sentence -- "Water waves,
% sound waves, and waves on a rope are all examples of mechanical waves." --
% was this table's `source`, the field that carries the tier, for ALL TEN rows.
% So `? wave_family(gamma, $F)` came back proved by a sentence that names no
% electromagnetic band at all. Seven of the ten rows were in that position.
%
% BOTH SENTENCES ARE ON THE SAME PAGE, so the locator was never wrong here and
% a hostname assertion could never have caught it -- only the span was. That is
% why this table's shipped test passed unchanged through the conversion.
%
% BLOCK STRUCTURE WAS CHECKED BEFORE ANY SPAN WAS CUT: the two membership
% sentences are the tails of two `<p>` figure captions, and the definition
% sentence opens one of them, after a "(Left) " marker that is NOT part of the
% span. Ordinary prose, no list items. (The check exists because flattened text
% hides the difference -- on the SEER cell-cycle page a span that read as a cut
% sentence turned out to be a complete paragraph followed by an ordered list.)
%
% THE ENVELOPE IS THE MECHANICAL-WAVE DEFINITION, and that choice is DISCLOSED
% rather than dressed up as neutral framing: this page frames each FAMILY
% separately and has no sentence framing the table as a whole. What the framing
% slot requires is that the envelope not MIS-WARRANT a row, and this sentence
% names no specific wave -- checked against all ten row keys in the page's own
% wording, where `xray` is "X-rays" and `rope` is "waves on a rope".
%
% All three spans were confirmed verbatim on the page, each with a negative arm
% (final word altered) absent. The two MEMBERSHIP sentences occur exactly once
% in the whole document. The DEFINITION sentence occurs once in the page's
% prose and four more times in `<head>` metadata -- meta description,
% og:description, twitter:description and the JSON-LD description, all copies
% of the same caption. Stated because "exactly once" was the first wording and
% it is true of the prose, not of the document.
%
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts, feedback_no_byte_arithmetic_for_llm.)
% ============================================================================

table wave_family {
    columns wave, family

    % Each row carries the sentence that NAMES IT. Two sentences, both on
    % the one NASA page: the mechanical caption names water, sound and
    % rope; the electromagnetic caption names all seven bands. Until
    % #14986 all ten rows carried the MECHANICAL sentence, which names no
    % electromagnetic band at all.
    row (water, mechanical) {
        source "Water waves, sound waves, and waves on a rope are all examples of mechanical waves."
        locator "https://science.nasa.gov/asset/webb/electromagnetic-wave-light-wave-vs-mechanical-wave/"
    }
    row (sound, mechanical) {
        source "Water waves, sound waves, and waves on a rope are all examples of mechanical waves."
        locator "https://science.nasa.gov/asset/webb/electromagnetic-wave-light-wave-vs-mechanical-wave/"
    }
    row (rope, mechanical) {
        source "Water waves, sound waves, and waves on a rope are all examples of mechanical waves."
        locator "https://science.nasa.gov/asset/webb/electromagnetic-wave-light-wave-vs-mechanical-wave/"
    }
    row (gamma, electromagnetic) {
        source "Light waves include all forms of electromagnetic radiation: gamma rays, X-rays, ultraviolet light, visible light, infrared light, microwaves, and radio waves."
        locator "https://science.nasa.gov/asset/webb/electromagnetic-wave-light-wave-vs-mechanical-wave/"
    }
    row (xray, electromagnetic) {
        source "Light waves include all forms of electromagnetic radiation: gamma rays, X-rays, ultraviolet light, visible light, infrared light, microwaves, and radio waves."
        locator "https://science.nasa.gov/asset/webb/electromagnetic-wave-light-wave-vs-mechanical-wave/"
    }
    row (ultraviolet, electromagnetic) {
        source "Light waves include all forms of electromagnetic radiation: gamma rays, X-rays, ultraviolet light, visible light, infrared light, microwaves, and radio waves."
        locator "https://science.nasa.gov/asset/webb/electromagnetic-wave-light-wave-vs-mechanical-wave/"
    }
    row (visible_light, electromagnetic) {
        source "Light waves include all forms of electromagnetic radiation: gamma rays, X-rays, ultraviolet light, visible light, infrared light, microwaves, and radio waves."
        locator "https://science.nasa.gov/asset/webb/electromagnetic-wave-light-wave-vs-mechanical-wave/"
    }
    row (infrared, electromagnetic) {
        source "Light waves include all forms of electromagnetic radiation: gamma rays, X-rays, ultraviolet light, visible light, infrared light, microwaves, and radio waves."
        locator "https://science.nasa.gov/asset/webb/electromagnetic-wave-light-wave-vs-mechanical-wave/"
    }
    row (microwave, electromagnetic) {
        source "Light waves include all forms of electromagnetic radiation: gamma rays, X-rays, ultraviolet light, visible light, infrared light, microwaves, and radio waves."
        locator "https://science.nasa.gov/asset/webb/electromagnetic-wave-light-wave-vs-mechanical-wave/"
    }
    row (radio, electromagnetic) {
        source "Light waves include all forms of electromagnetic radiation: gamma rays, X-rays, ultraviolet light, visible light, infrared light, microwaves, and radio waves."
        locator "https://science.nasa.gov/asset/webb/electromagnetic-wave-light-wave-vs-mechanical-wave/"
    }

    % THE ENVELOPE IS THE MECHANICAL-WAVE DEFINITION, and that choice is
    % disclosed rather than dressed up as neutral framing: this page
    % frames each FAMILY separately and has no sentence framing the table
    % as a whole. What the framing slot requires is that the envelope not
    % MIS-WARRANT a row, and this sentence names no specific wave —
    % checked against all ten row keys in the page's own wording, where
    % `xray` is "X-rays" and `rope` is "waves on a rope".
    source "A mechanical wave, also called a matter wave, is a propagation of energy through matter."
    locator "https://science.nasa.gov/asset/webb/electromagnetic-wave-light-wave-vs-mechanical-wave/"
    trust authoritative
}
